sec.p:
In this study, we focused on the excitatory-inhibitory rWW model with FIC35,36. Here, each node consists of an excitatory (E) and an inhibitory (I) neuronal ensemble that interact with each other, modulated by a set of parameters: wip\begin{document}$w_{i}^{p}$\end{document} (local excitatory recurrence), JiN\begin{document}$J_{i}^{N}$\end{document} (NMDA receptor conductance), wiIE\begin{document}$w_{i}^{I E}$\end{document} (inhibitory to excitatory connection weight) and wII (local inhibitory recurrence). The nodes are connected to each other through their E neuronal ensembles according to the SC weights (Cij; connectivity strength of node j to node i) scaled by global coupling G as well as JiN\begin{document}$J_{i}^{N}$\end{document}. Additive Gaussian noise (ϵiE,ϵiI)\begin{document}$\left(\epsilon_{i}^{E}, \epsilon_{i}^{I}\right)$\end{document} drives stochastic fluctuations and its magnitude is controlled by σi. The node dynamics for a given node i is described by the following equations:

(1)

IiE=WEIb+wipJiNSiE+GJiN∑jCijSjE−wiIESiI
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{i}^{E}=W^{E} I_{b}+w_{i}^{p} J_{i}^{N} S_{i}^{E}+G J_{i}^{N} \sum_{j} C_{i j} S_{j}^{E}-w_{i}^{I E} S_{i}^{I} \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(2)

IiI=WIIb+JiNSiE−wIISiI
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{i}^{I}=W^{I} I_{b}+J_{i}^{N} S_{i}^{E}-w^{I I} S_{i}^{I} \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(3)

riE=HE(IiE)=aEIiE−bE1−e−dE(aEIiE−bE)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{i}^{E}=H^{E}\left(I_{i}^{E}\right)=\frac{a^{E} I_{i}^{E}-b^{E}}{1-e^{-d^{E}\left(a^{E} I_{i}^{E}-b^{E}\right)}} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(4)

riI=HI(IiI)=aIIiI−bI1−e−dI(aIIiI−bI)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{i}^{I}=H^{I}\left(I_{i}^{I}\right)=\frac{a^{I} I_{i}^{I}-b^{I}}{1-e^{-d^{I}\left(a^{I} I_{i}^{I}-b^{I}\right)}} \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(5)

SiE˙=−SiEτE+(1−SiE)γriE(t)+σiϵiE
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \dot{S_{i}^{E}}=-\frac{S_{i}^{E}}{\tau_{E}}+\left(1-S_{i}^{E}\right) \gamma r_{i}^{E}(t)+\sigma_{i} \epsilon_{i}^{E} \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(6)

S˙iI=−SiIτI+riI+σiϵiI
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \dot{S}_{i}^{I}=-\frac{S_{i}^{I}}{\tau_{I}}+r_{i}^{I}+\sigma_{i} \epsilon_{i}^{I} \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



    Here, Ii(E/I)\begin{document}$I_{i}^{(E / I)}$\end{document} (input current [nA] to the E or I ensemble), ri(E/I)\begin{document}$r_{i}^{(E / I)}$\end{document} (firing rate [Hz] of E or I ensembles) and Si(E/I)\begin{document}$S_{i}^{(E / I)}$\end{document} (synaptic gating variable of E or I ensembles) are the model state variables for each node i. Notably, Si(E/I)\begin{document}$S_{i}^{(E / I)}$\end{document} is restricted to the interval [0, 1]. The parameters used in the equations, along with their values, are summarized in Table S2.

The neural activity of each node derived from the BNM, can subsequently be transformed to an observation-level signal using a forward model. Here, we used the hemodynamic model of Balloon-Windkessel to simulate node-wise BOLD signal. In each BNM, one of the state variables is selected to represent the neural activity of a given node. In the rWW model, SiE\begin{document}$S_{i}^{E}$\end{document} (t) is used for this purpose and serves as an input to the Balloon-Windkessel model, described by the following system of differential equations:
(9)
xi˙=SiE−κxi(t)−γ(fi−1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \dot{x_{i}}=S_{i}^{E}-\kappa x_{i}(t)-\gamma\left(f_{i}-1\right) \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(10)
fi=xi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{i}=x_{i} \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(11)
τνi˙=fi−νi1α \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau \dot{\nu_{i}}=f_{i}-\nu_{i}^{\frac{1}{\alpha}} \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(12)
τq˙i=fiρ[1−((1−ρ)1fi)]−qiν1ανi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau \dot{q}_{i}=\frac{f_{i}}{\rho}\left[1-\left((1-\rho)^{\frac{1}{f_{i}}}\right)\right]-\frac{q_{i} \nu^{\frac{1}{\alpha}}}{\nu_{i}} \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where xi (vasodilatory signal), fi (blood inflow), νi (blood volume) and qi (deoxyhemoglobin content) are the model state variables in node i, and κ=10.65s−1\begin{document}$\kappa=\frac{1}{0.65} \mathrm{~s}^{-1}$\end{document} is the rate of signal decay, γ=10.41s−1\begin{document}$\gamma=\frac{1}{0.41} \mathrm{~s}^{-1}$\end{document} is the rate of flow-dependent elimination, τ = 0.98 s is the hemodynamic transmit time, α = 0.32 is the Grubb’s exponent, and ρ = 0.34 is the resting oxygen extraction fraction. Subsequently, the BOLD signal is calculated at every TR (0.72 s for the HCP-YA dataset) as:
(13)
BOLDi=V0[k1(1−qi)+k2(1−qiνi)+k3(1−νi)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} B O L D_{i}=V_{0}\left[k_{1}\left(1-q_{i}\right)+k_{2}\left(1-\frac{q_{i}}{\nu_{i}}\right)+k_{3}\left(1-\nu_{i}\right)\right] \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
in which V0 = 2% is the resting blood volume fraction and k1 = 3.72, k2 = 0.527 and k3 = 0.53 are derived for 3T scans36,75.
